# Line-ending policy.
#
# # Why this file exists
#
# Without it, git applies its own default text conversion, and on a Windows checkout that rewrites
# `\n` into `\r\n` on the way to disk. For source that is harmless. For a file whose whole purpose
# is to be compared **byte for byte** it is not: the comparison then measures the checkout rather
# than the program.
#
# That is not hypothetical. `crates/renvor-cli/tests/json/*.json` records what each JSON envelope
# looked like before contract C-8 existed, and `tests/presentation.rs` asserts the shipped binary
# still emits those exact bytes. On the Windows leg of the matrix both toolchains failed with the
# document differing only in line endings — the binary emitting `\n`, the fixture arriving as
# `\r\n`. The envelope was identical on every platform; the fixture was not.

# THE DEFAULT: normalise to LF in the repository, and check out LF everywhere.
#
# This project has no Windows-only text file that needs CRLF, and every consumer of these files —
# rustc, cargo, lychee, the docs toolchain — reads LF happily on all three platforms. Pinning it
# means a file's bytes are the same wherever it is read, which is the property the expected-output
# tests rest on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# BYTE-EXACT FIXTURES. Not text at all, as far as git is concerned.
#
# `-text` is stronger than `eol=lf`: it disables conversion entirely rather than choosing a
# direction, so no future `core.autocrlf` setting on a contributor's machine can reach them.
crates/renvor-cli/tests/json/*.json -text

# Expected-output snapshots, for the same reason. `trycmd` normalises line endings itself, so
# these were never the failure — but they are the same kind of artifact and there is no reason for
# them to depend on a tool's leniency.
crates/renvor-cli/tests/cmd/*.trycmd -text
